{"answer": "The single factor that contributed most to the decline in global extreme poverty since 1990 was rapid, sustained economic growth in Asia — most concentrated in China, whose growth alone lifted roughly 940 million Chinese people out of extreme poverty since 1990 (Our World in Data / World Bank Poverty and Inequality Platform). Mechanism: sustained per-capita income growth from export-led industrialization and market reforms (starting with China's reforms, and trade integration across Asia) raised labor productivity and wages above the $3/day international poverty line far faster than transfers or aid could.

Strongest evidence: World Bank data show global extreme poverty fell from 43% of the world population in 1990 to about 10% by 2025. Crucially, the counterfactual test — excluding China — still shows a large decline (33% in 1990 to 12% in 2025), demonstrating that the mechanism is growth-led poverty reduction generally, with China as its largest single contributor given that roughly one in six people on Earth lives there (Our World in Data, 'Was the global decline of extreme poverty only due to China?', Feb 21, 2026, https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/was-the-global-decline-of-extreme-poverty-only-due-to-china). The World Bank's Poverty, Prosperity, and Planet Report 2024 likewise attributes the post-1990 escape of more than 1 billion people from extreme poverty primarily to rapid economic growth, especially in China and India (https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/server/api/core/bitstreams/f75dd18d-4e3f-44f9-b455-7f0d8e189609/content).

Calibration: The claim that China is the largest single-country contributor is well-evidenced and low-uncertainty (hundreds of millions more people exited poverty in China than in any other country over the period). The framing of 'economic growth' as the factor rather than, e.g., 'trade integration' is a matter of aggregation level; growth is the proximate mechanism cited by both the World Bank and Our World in Data. Attribution of the non-China residual (33%→12%) across India and other developing countries carries moderate uncertainty due to survey-coverage limitations, but the direction and magnitude of the overall decline are firmly established."}

