  "answer": "As of 16 November 2026, the projected impact of CBDCs on commercial-bank liquidity and monetary-policy transmission in the G7 between 2027 and 2030 is best understood as a story of divergence and design, not of a single launch event — because no G7 economy is on track to have a live retail CBDC in mass circulation for most of that window. The eurozone is furthest along: the ECB's preparation phase ran from November 2023 to October 2025 and the Governing Council has moved to the next phase, but issuance depends on EU lawmakers adopting the digital euro regulation in 2026, in which case the ECB says the digital euro could be issued during 2029 (https://eco.com/support/en/articles/15210561-cbdc-updates-2026-what-changed-this-year?ref=hackernoon.com). The UK remains in a design phase with no decision to build taken; the Bank of England and HM Treasury are due to publish a blueprint and joint assessment ahead of a decision on next steps in 2026, with any digital pound holding cap discussed at £10,000–£20,000 per person and earliest issuance in the second half of the decade (https://eco.com/support/en/articles/15210561-cbdc-updates-2026-what-changed-this-year?ref=hackernoon.com). The United States has gone the other way: Executive Order 14178 (January 2025) bars a Fed-issued retail CBDC, and the GENIUS Act (July 2025) instead created a federal framework for payment stablecoins, so US digital money in 2027–2030 will run through regulated private stablecoins rather than central-bank money (https://eco.com/support/en/articles/15210561-cbdc-updates-2026-what-changed-this-year?ref=hackernoon.com). Japan and Canada remain in research/pilot mode with no retail launch committed, so the realistic G7 baseline for 2027–2030 is: at most one retail CBDC (the digital euro, late in the window), no US retail CBDC, and wholesale experimentation as the main live channel.

Liquidity impact — commercial banks: the literature distinguishes 'slow' from 'fast' disintermediation. In normal times a retail CBDC competes with deposits as a close substitute, raising bank funding costs and shrinking the low-cost deposit base that funds mortgages and business loans; in stress it offers a risk-free asset that can be held in large volumes at no cost, accelerating digital bank runs (https://www.neweconomyreview.com/macro-trends/how-cbdcs-could-reshape-global-finance-design-choices-not-technology-will-decide). The 2027–2030 impact is therefore capped by design, not by demand: the digital euro and digital pound are both planned as non-interest-bearing (or tiered-remuneration) instruments with holding limits, distributed through banks as intermediaries, precisely to choke off the fast-disintermediation channel. A 2026 panel/DSGE study finds deposit substitution is non-linear — modest below roughly 12% adoption, more than doubling above it — and that interest-bearing CBDCs above about 50bp threaten loan-to-deposit ratios, which is why G7 designs avoid remuneration. Net projection: a slow, contained drain of transactional balances (low single-digit percent of deposits in the euro area by 2030 under the caps), not a funding crisis; banks offset it with higher deposit rates and wholesale funding, compressing net interest margins rather than credit volumes. The larger liquidity story is wholesale: Project Agora (BIS Innovation Hub with seven major central banks including the Bank of England, Bank of Japan and the New York Fed) is testing tokenised commercial-bank deposits interoperating with tokenised central-bank money on a shared programmable platform, with technical experimentation running through 2026 (https://eco.com/support/en/articles/15210561-cbdc-updates-2026-what-changed-this-year?ref=hackernoon.com) — if that moves to production in 2027–2030 it would improve intraday and cross-border settlement liquidity for banks without touching retail deposits at all.

Monetary-policy transmission: an interest-bearing CBDC would strengthen pass-through (households holding a remunerated central-bank liability force banks to compete for deposits, so policy-rate changes feed through to deposit rates faster), but the G7 designs now in play are non-remunerated, so that channel will be weak before 2030 (https://www.neweconomyreview.com/macro-trends/how-cbdcs-could-reshape-global-finance-design-choices-not-technology-will-decide). Instead, transmission improves through a different route: a CBDC gives the central bank a direct, programmable settlement asset and a real-time view of payment flows, and wholesale tokenisation (Agora) shortens settlement lags. The risk is asymmetric: in stress, flight into CBDC could impair bank lending and therefore weaken transmission itself, which is the core reason holding limits exist. The US split matters for transmission too: with no retail CBDC, the Fed's transmission stays bank-intermediated, while stablecoin growth under the GENIUS Act creates a parallel dollar payment layer outside the banking system — a transmission leak the Fed cannot directly steer.

Bottom line for 2027–2030: (1) liquidity — contained, margin-compressing disintermediation in the euro area from ~2029, negligible in the UK/US before 2030, with wholesale tokenisation the bigger liquidity-positive story; (2) transmission — modest strengthening via programmability and faster settlement, not via remuneration, and a new stress-channel risk managed by holding caps; (3) the binding constraint is politics and legislation, not technology — the digital euro's 2029 date, the UK's 2026 decision, and the US prohibition are the three variables that decide whether any of this materialises in the window. The main uncertainty is adoption: the three live retail CBDCs (Bahamas, Jamaica, Nigeria) all show that launching is easy and getting citizens to use it instead of incumbent rails is hard (https://eco.com/support/en/articles/15210561-cbdc-updates-2026-what-changed-this-year?ref=hackernoon.com), so even a launched digital euro could have a smaller liquidity footprint than its design allows."
